{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Elohim saw, etc.</b> Meaning: through the creation of man, His work was seen in its totality to be good — for if there is no man, what benefit is there in all the order and in all of creation and the plants of the earth? For the quality of good is not recognized except through the agency of man, for to him the good is recognized and examined, and he gives thanks to the One who bestows it. And His saying the word <b>and behold</b> — which He did not precisely say concerning the days regarding which He said ״כִּי טוֹב״ — is because man, we find, corrupted his deeds and did evil. Therefore He precisely said <b>and behold</b>, for what existed then, from the side of HaShem's deed, was very good. And afterward, man sought many calculations, because free choice was in his hand to do evil or to do good, and he negated the good and brought about the aspect of evil in his deeds. But from the side of HaShem's deed, He created nothing but for the side of good." "Further, it intends in His saying <b>and Elohim saw</b> etc., that here He alluded that at the time of man's creation, HaShem looked upon the righteous (Genesis Rabbah 3), and this is His saying <b>and He saw</b> etc. <b>and behold, good</b> — these are the righteous. And although He looked upon the wicked, for them too He prepared and made a remedy for their creation, and this is His saying <b>very</b> (me'od). And they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 9): ״מְאֹד״ — this is the Angel of Death, through whose agency the created beings who deviate from the path of reason are set right. And therefore, corresponding to the aspect of the righteous, He said <b>and behold, good</b> — meaning, behold, they are an aspect of good without any need for death. And corresponding to the aspect of those who persist in their perverseness, they too exist in an aspect of good through the agency of me'od:" "<b>Further,</b> He alludes here to the reason for creation — for we have learned that the essence of everything is man, and for his sake and because of him HaShem created all His creations. From this a question now comes to man: why? Therefore He said <b>and behold, very good</b> — meaning that even though the aspect of man's soul is from the aspect of good, despite all this HaShem created him in order to ascend through levels of good beyond what he already is. And this is His saying <b>very</b> — through man's deeds his soul is elevated from level to level. Happy is the man who does this!"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:57:57.192530797Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}